00:37:42.000 --> 00:38:30.000
Shannon Lev Levy and I recently published an article outlining this idea that maybe what they're doing is a lot more important and sophisticated than we're giving them credit for. Because we think in their peer groups, They are not just playing and socializing, but they are actually producing culture. There's actually a term we borrow from sociology called peer culture, and it's the body of cultural information generated and transmitted between children. And you might think of your own experiences of pure culture, for instance, in the West, is like little playground games or, you know, little hand-clapping games or rhymes or whatever. And that's true. Like I think a lot of the peer culture in West encompasses these things about play. But we did this broader analysis of peer culture,
阐述了这样一种观点：也许他们在做的事情比我们给予的认可要重要得多、复杂得多。因为我们认为在他们的同伴群体中，他们不仅在玩耍和社交，而且实际上还在生产文化。我们从社会学中借用了一个术语叫做“同伴文化”，它是儿童之间生成和传播的文化信息主体。你可能会想到自己在西方经历过的同伴文化，例如小操场游戏、拍手游戏、顺口溜等等。这确实没错，我认为西方许多同伴文化确实包含这些关于游戏的内容。但我们对同伴文化进行了更广泛的分析，

56
00:38:30.000 --> 00:39:12.000
and what you find is that in a lot of other societies, especially these foraging societies, it is much more complex and much broader than that. Children have lots of cultural information, for instance, about how to forage for food, they target different game, they make their own material culture, they pick up the social and linguistic conventions of groups around them. They're much more exploratory. And one thing that we're arguing is that this kind of exploratory and distinct culture is actually really important in the history of cultural evolution, because it can help us as a whole community adapt when environments change. So as an adult, you're maybe setting your ways, you're doing things that you've probably been doing for 20 years.
你会发现，在许多其他社会中，尤其是这些以采集为生的社会，情况要复杂得多，也广泛得多。儿童拥有大量的文化信息，例如关于如何采集食物、他们会锁定不同的猎物、他们会制造自己的物质文化、他们会吸收周围群体的社会和语言习俗。他们的探索性要强得多。我们的一个观点是，这种探索性和独特的文化在文化进化的历史上其实非常重要，因为当环境发生变化时，它能够帮助整个社群进行适应。作为一个成年人，你可能已经墨守成规，做着你可能已经做了20年的事情。

